problem:
3. A square piece of paper is folded in half along a line of symmetry. The resulting shape is then folded in half along a line of symmetry of the new shape. This process is repeated until $n$ folds have been made, giving a sequence of $n+1$ shapes. If we do not distinguish between congruent shapes, find the number of possible sequences when:
(a) $n=3$;
(b) $n=6$;
(c) $n=9$.
(When $n=1$ there are two possible sequences.)

Problem 10.1. Let \( f(x) = x^{2} - a x + a^{2} - 4 \), where \( a \) is a real parameter. Find the values of \( a \) for which:
a) the equation \( f(x) = 0 \) has real roots \( x_{1} \) and \( x_{2} \) (not necessarily distinct) such that \( \left| x_{1}^{3} - x_{2}^{3} \right| \leq 4 \);
b) the inequality \( f(x) \geq 0 \) holds for every integer \( x \).

ground_truth_solution:
9.2. Note that the system makes sense for $x \geq 0, y \geq 0$ and $x+y>0$ and has no solutions for which $x=0$ or $y=0$. From now on, we will consider $x$ and $y$ to be positive. We sequentially obtain

$$
\left|\begin{array}{l}
1+\frac{a}{x+y}=\frac{2}{\sqrt{2 x}} \\
1-\frac{a}{x+y}=\frac{2}{\sqrt{3 y}}
\end{array} \Longleftrightarrow\right| \begin{aligned}
& 1=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2 x}}+\frac{1}{\sqrt{3 y}} \\
& \frac{a}{x+y}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2 x}}-\frac{1}{\sqrt{3 y}}
\end{aligned}
$$

We multiply the last equations and obtain $\frac{a}{x+y}=\frac{1}{2 x}-\frac{1}{3 y}$, from which we arrive at the homogeneous equation $2 x^{2}+(6 a-1) x y-3 y^{2}=0$.

Let us set $t=\frac{x}{y}$. Then the rational positive number $t$ is a root of the quadratic equation $2 t^{2}+(6 a-1) t-3=0$, which has integer coefficients. It is easy to see that we have four possibilities: $t=\frac{3}{2}, \frac{1}{2}, 3,1$.

$$
\text { For } t=\frac{3}{2}, \text { i.e. } 2 x=3 y, \text { we obtain } a=0,1=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2 x}}+\frac{1}{\sqrt{3 y}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2 x}},
$$

from which $x=2, y=\frac{4}{3}$. For $t=\frac{1}{2}$, i.e. $2 x=y$, we obtain $a=1,1=$ $\frac{1}{\sqrt{y}}+\frac{1}{\sqrt{3 y}}$, which has no rational solution. For $t=1$ and $t=3$ we obtain the respective values $a=\frac{1}{3}$ and $a=-\frac{2}{3}$, which are not integers. Therefore, $a=0$ is the only solution.

